<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml">

    <!--
        Metrics shared by both surface families. Colour lives in Controls.Tavern.xaml,
        Controls.Parchment.xaml and Controls.Semantic.xaml, all of which derive from the styles
        below via BasedOn. A padding or radius change belongs here so it lands once rather than
        once per derived file.

        ADOPTION IS NOW COMPLETE AND RATCHETED. Every Style key in this file and its three
        derivatives has at least one call site outside a comment, and
        ThemeDictionaryTests.NoOrphanedStyleKeys fails the build if that stops being true. DO
        NOT ADD A STYLE HERE "for later": the previous revision of this comment was a hand
        census of 17 dead keys, it was WRONG (it counted DmBorderTavernStyle as live on the
        strength of a mention inside this very comment), and the styles it was tracking were
        eventually deleted rather than adopted.

        The keys that no longer exist, and why, are recorded in the file that used to declare
        each: Controls.Tavern.xaml lost five, Controls.Parchment.xaml two, and this file lost
        DmSearchBarBase (there is no SearchBar element in the app at all) and DmPickerBase
        (its two derivatives went, and the one Picker is on a tool-panel surface that is
        neither family).

        THE TWO TRAPS THAT GOVERN ANY FUTURE ADOPTION, both hit during this sweep:

          1. A VisualState setter OUTRANKS AN IMPERATIVE ASSIGNMENT. DmConfirmButton's Normal
             state sets BackgroundColor, and DmConfirmPopup used to switch its confirm button
             between green and red by assigning BackgroundColor in code-behind. Putting the two
             together would have rendered EVERY DESTRUCTIVE DIALOG GREEN, with a clean build
             and no warning. The fix is to swap the STYLE — the popup now assigns
             DmTheme.DestructiveButton or DmTheme.ConfirmButton — not to fight the state.
          2. DmBorderBase sets BackgroundColor to Transparent, so a Border with a surface of
             its own must restate it when it adopts a paired style. Four do.
    -->

    <Style x:Key="DmButtonBase" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontTitle}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeAction}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource DmRadiusDefault}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderWidth" Value="{StaticResource DmStrokeHairline}" />
        <!-- Padding and the minimum size requests deliberately match the geometry-only
             implicit Button style in Implicit.xaml, so a themed button and an
             unstyled one settle on the same footprint instead of fighting each
             other. CornerRadius stays bound to DmRadiusDefault: a button that
             opts into a keyed style is opting into the themed 3px radius on
             purpose. -->
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,10" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumHeightRequest" Value="{StaticResource DmTouchTargetMinimum}" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumWidthRequest" Value="{StaticResource DmTouchTargetMinimum}" />
        <Setter Property="VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups">
            <VisualStateGroupList>
                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.82" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.7" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                    <!-- This Disabled state is deliberate and it is OPT-IN: a keyed style is
                         chosen at the call site, so a Button carrying one has asked for the
                         themed disabled treatment. Implicit.xaml has no Disabled state at all,
                         and its comment records why — a VisualState setter outranks BOTH a
                         DataTrigger and a local value, so an implicit one would silently
                         overrule every call site that dims itself.
                         The same precedence applies here: this 0.45 outranks anything a
                         consuming Button sets for itself. Nothing is affected today. Of the
                         three live consumers, DmConfirmButton restates the whole group, and
                         neither DmNeutralButton (both CANCEL buttons) nor DmDestructiveButton
                         (DmConfirmPopup's confirm button in destructive mode) is ever disabled.
                         A future keyed style that wants its own disabled appearance must
                         restate this whole group, not add a setter at the call site. -->
                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.45" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                </VisualStateGroup>
            </VisualStateGroupList>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmEntryBase" TargetType="Entry">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeBody}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmEditorBase" TargetType="Editor">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeBody}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="AutoSize" Value="Disabled" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmLabelBase" TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeBody}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmBorderBase" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="{StaticResource DmStrokeHairline}" />
        <!-- The 3 mirrors DmRadiusDefault and must move with it;
             BorderBaseStrokeShapeRadius_EqualsDmRadiusDefault enforces that. The STRING form
             used here cannot avoid the literal: its converter parses a shape name and its
             arguments out of one string and has nowhere to accept a markup extension.

             THE STRING FORM IS KEPT HERE DELIBERATELY, AND THE REASON IS NOT "the token will
             not bind". It binds. The element form puts the radius back in attribute position,
             where a StaticResource resolves — that was spiked on a Border ELEMENT and proved at
             runtime (see the note beside the radius tokens in Tokens.xaml), and every Border
             element in Controls/ now uses it.

             The reason is the SETTER, which is a different case and was spiked separately on
             2026-08-07. A Style setter holding an element-form shape creates the shape ONCE,
             when the dictionary is parsed, and hands the SAME INSTANCE to every Border that
             adopts the style. Measured, not reasoned, by giving this setter an element-form
             RoundRectangle and applying the style to two Borders:

               ReferenceEquals(a.StrokeShape, b.StrokeShape)  ->  True  (identical hash codes)
               a.StrokeShape.Parent, b.StrokeShape.Parent     ->  BOTH report the SECOND Border

             Two consequences follow, and only the first is benign. Rendering is safe: the path
             is computed per call from the bounds the Border passes in, so unmutated, both
             Borders rendered the correct — and deliberately exaggerated — radius, confirmed by
             screenshot. But the instance is shared MUTABLE state: setting CornerRadius through
             one Border changed what the OTHER Border rendered, observed on screen in the same
             frame. And the shape's logical Parent is hijacked by whichever Border adopted the
             style last, so anything the shape ever needs to inherit — a BindingContext, a
             resource lookup — would resolve against the wrong element.

             A string-form literal has neither problem: the converter builds a fresh shape per
             Border. So this setter keeps its literal, and the guard keeps it honest. Do not
             "finish the migration" by converting it.

             This setter is a default, not a constraint, and must not be removed on the grounds
             that it "forces" a radius. MAUI value precedence puts a locally-set property above
             a Style setter, so a Border may carry Style="{StaticResource DmBorderTavernStyle}"
             AND its own StrokeShape="RoundRectangle 4" attribute — or a <Border.StrokeShape>
             element carrying a custom geometry — and keep its own shape. Adopting a paired
             style alongside a different shape is intended usage; CreateDungeonPopup.xaml does
             exactly this. -->
        <Setter Property="StrokeShape" Value="RoundRectangle 3" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
